#* {{quote-book|en|year=1899| title=The Development of Our Town Government|author=T Frank Waters| page=| passage=By order of the General Court in 1642, the "prudentiall" men of each town were instructed "to take care of such [children] as are sett to keep cattle be set to some other employment withal, as spinning upon the '''rock''', knitting, weaving tape, etc., and that boys and girls be not suffered to converse together so as may occasion any wanton, dishonest or immodest behavior.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1902| title=The Encyclopaedia Britannica|author=Day Otis Kellogg; Thomas Spencer Baynes; William Robertson Smith | page=664| passage=A prepared end of yarn being fixed into the notch, the spinster, by a smart rolling motion of the spindle with the right hand against the right leg, threw it out from her, spinning in the air, while, with the left hand, she drew from the '''rock''' an additional supply of fibre which was formed into a uniform and equal strand with the right.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920 | title=The Linen Trade of Europe During the Spinning-wheel Period |author=John Horner | page=32| passage=It is true that in Ireland, even in recent years, the flax, before being placed on the '''rock''' or distaff, was tangled into a mass, or, as Cormmelin expresses it, “drawn out in a flat cake.” }}
